## 2. The hypothesis that was wrong, and why it is worth recording

`actor_loss` was displaying as `-0.000000` and this was read as "the actor is
not learning". It is not a bug. It is arithmetic:

```
MINI-EPOCH 1, on-policy
  ratio  mean 1.000000   min 1.0000  max 1.0000
  adv_n  mean 0.000000   std 1.0000
  actor  -0.000000
```

On the first mini-epoch the policy has not changed, so `ratio` is exactly 1;
advantages are normalised, so their mean is exactly 0. `-mean(1 x adv_n)` is
zero by construction in **every correct PPO implementation**. The displayed
actor loss carries no information about progress and should not be read as
if it does.

Second hypothesis, also wrong: that the critic was swamping the actor on the
shared trunk. Measured, rather than assumed:

```
fc1.weight   actor 6.425498e-02   critic 2.136788e-01   critic 3x
fc2.weight   actor 1.029662e-01   critic 4.448902e-01   critic 4x
```

3–4x is normal for a shared trunk, not pathological. The actor gradient was
healthy the whole time.

**Cost of this detour: about an hour.** The cheaper move would have been to
check which file the GUI actually loads before theorising about gradients.
Recorded here so the next person checks the boring thing first.

