## 9. Parent/child training — noted, not built

Proposed: train a parent that only wins on the shortest route; run it again
recording its actions; train a child guided by those actions, but free to
deviate so obedience can be observed.

This is **behavioural cloning followed by RL fine-tuning** — how AlphaGo
started and how VPT learned Minecraft. It works.

Known failure mode: **distribution shift.** The child only sees states the
parent visited. The moment it deviates it is somewhere the parent never
demonstrated, with no guidance, and small errors compound. The fix has a
name — **DAgger** — and is a small change: let the child drive, and when it
reaches an unfamiliar state, query the parent for what it would have done
and add that to the training set. The child then learns recovery, not just
the route. Cheap here because the parent is a live network, not a fixed
recording.

**Ordering constraint:** cloning a wanderer produces a wanderer, faster. The
parent has to be good first, which routes straight back to §6.

Selective skill inheritance was raised and correctly flagged as hard: skills
are not stored separably — "knows hazards hurt" is smeared across the whole
network. Open research problem, not an implementation detail.

